in this lesson we're going to talk about  the law of cosines now the law of  cosines has shown up on previous act  tests and usually when it does they give  you the formula so it's not something  that you have to memorize but it's  something that you need to know how to  use so  when you use the law of cosines it's  when you have a triangle that's not a  right triangle so it doesn't have that  right angle in there and you know to use  the law of cosines when you are only  given three sides side side side or just  side angle side you have the angle in  between the two known lengths of those  sides  so here's the three different forms  you can usually just memorize one of  them is what i do but you can see the  pattern here you've got c squared equals  a squared plus b squared just like  pythagorean theorem minus two a b cosine  c now notice the side c and the angle c  they're across from each other in the  formula they're also across from each  other in the triangle so in the triangle  the capital letters are the angles and  then the sides across from those angles  those are the sides and you can see  that's given a lowercase letter so you  can see angle a side a angle c side c  okay so same thing here you can see in  this variation of the form they have  side b and angle b okay they're across  from each other in the triangle and  they're also across from each other in  the formula and then you can see a and c  a and c those are the two sides that  make up that angle same thing here  you've got side a and angle a okay  they're across from each other in the  triangle across from each other in the  formula and then the other two  quantities b and c b and c those are the  two sides that make up that angle so  let's go through an example you can see  how this works so  i usually give you an example like this  where they have you find the distance  between two objects say for example you  have two planes they leave the airport  at the same time at a 10 degree angle  from each other one travels two miles  one travels three miles how far apart  are they uh go ahead and find that  distance so pause the video see if you  can do this one we'll go through it  together  so in this example let's just use this  first formula here so let's just say  that this is side c here this is angle c  here and then what we'll do is we'll  make this uh  angle b and we'll make this side b and  we'll make this angle a will make this  side a so what we're going to do is  we're going to use this first formula  right here okay  so what we have is we're trying to look  for c this length here so we've got c  squared equals  a squared which is the 3  mile squared plus the b squared which is  a 2 mile squared  minus 2 times 3 times 2  times the cosine of the angle that's in  between the two sides that they're  giving us so that's  10 degrees so what we're going to do is  we're going to input this on our  calculator and then because we're just  looking for c what we're going to do is  we're going to take the square root of  both sides so i'm going to go ahead and  do that now we can check our answer here  together in a moment so it's going to be  let's see 3 squared is 9 plus 2 squared  is 4 minus 2 times 3 times  2 times the cosine of 10 just make sure  your calculator is set to degrees since  this is in degrees  okay so that comes out to about 15.96  and if i take the square root of that  we're looking at 3.99 so i'm just going  to say approximately  four miles  so that's it that's the law of cosines  again just familiar familiarize yourself  with the formulas and just kind of see  the pattern that i was showing you here  originally about how the angle of side  are across from each other and then the  other two sides are what are going to be  these two quantities here see a and b a  and b or a and c a and c  so definitely something important to  understand i'll see in the next lesson
 

